===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[building#Noun|building]] or other [[structure#Noun|structure]] [[contain]]ing a ([[large]]) [[wheel#Noun|wheel]], such as the [[water wheel]] of a [[mill#Noun|mill]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Brick Machine|magazine=New-York Farmer, and American Gardener’s Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by the proprietor, D. K. Minor,{{nb...|No. 35 Wall Street.}}|month=February|year=1835|volume=III (New Series; volumne VIII overall)|issue=2|page=47|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=l3ZNAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA47|column=2|oclc=7527228|passage=A machine with two pair of moulds only, will make from fifty to seventy thousand bricks per week. But if the regular market be large, it can, by using a steam machine of 10 horse power, work sixteen moulds, (four on each side of the '''wheel house''',) and make two hundred thousand bricks per week.}}
## {{lb|en|automotive}} The [[partially]] [[enclosed#Adjective|enclosed]] structure above and around a wheel of an [[automobile]], typically partly [[form#Verb|formed]] by a portion of a [[fender]] [[panel#Noun|panel]] that has been [[extend]]ed outward beyond the [[plane#Noun|plane]] of the rest of the panel.
##: {{synonyms|en|wheel arch|wheel well}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} An enclosed [[compartment]] on the [[deck#Noun|deck]] of a [[vessel]] such as a [[fishing boat]], originally [[house#Verb|housing]] its [[helm#Noun|helm]] or [[steering wheel]], from which it may be [[navigate]]d; on a larger vessel it is the [[bridge#Noun|bridge]].
##: {{synonyms|en|pilothouse}}
##* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Explosion of the Steam-boat New-England, at Essex, Connecticut River, October 9th, 1833|title=The Mariners’ Chronicle: Containing Narratives of the Most Remarkable Disasters at Sea,{{nb...|such as Shipwrecks, Storms, Fires and Famines, also Naval Engagements, Piratical Adventures, Incidents of Discovery, and Other Extraordinary and Interesting Occurrences.}}|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher=Published by George W. Gorton|year=1835|page=471|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Hhp-8Vaxq08C&pg=RA2-PA475-IA4|oclc=7407361|passage=Captain Waterman was on the '''wheel-house''' at the time of the explosion, attending to the landing of passengers from the small boat. He noticed a movement over the boilers, and immediately jumped or was thrown upon the forward deck. He was somewhat bruised, but not seriously injured.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Jacob Abbott|authorlink=Jacob Abbott|chapter=Parallax|title=Rollo’s Philosophy. [Sky.]|series=The Rollo Series|edition=new revised|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Thomas Y. Crowell Co.|Thomas Y[oung] Crowell & Co.]]|year=1855|page=164|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gLEXAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA164|oclc=4274665|passage=The '''wheel-house''' is a small room or closet, with windows in front, built on the deck, in the forward part of the boat, where the helmsman stands to steer. The windows in the front of the '''wheel-house''' are for him to look out, and see where he is going. [...] There is a large wheel in this place, which is the reason why they call it the '''wheel-house'''. The wheel has handles to it, all around, for the man to take hold of, to turn the wheel one way or the other.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Norman L. Latson, witness|title=[Appendix I: Foreign Relations of the United States: 1894. Mosquito Territory.] Affidavit of N. L. Latson|journal=The Executive Documents of the House of Representatives for the Third Session of the Fifty-third Congress. 1894–95. In Thirty-five Volumes|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|Government Printing Office]]|date=22 September 1894|year_published=1895|page=345|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=r5xLgujeckMC&pg=PA345|oclc=156782073|passage=Affiant further states that thereupon Judge-Advocate Portocarrero, closely followed by Minister Madriz, rushed into the '''wheelhouse''' of the steamship ''Yulu''. They were both white with anger, and Portocarrero had in his right hand, with his finger on the spring, a clasp knife with a blade about 8 inches long.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Martin Barstad, witness|chapter=Evidence for the Government|title=John Andersen, Plaintiff in Error, vs. The United States: In Error to the Circuit Court of the United States for the Eastern District of Virginia: Transcript of Record|series={{w|Supreme Court of the United States}} (October Term, 1897)|seriesvolume=no. 583|date=18 December 1897|year_published=14 February 1898|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JSsrAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA3-PT95|passage=I last saw William Saunders, the mate of the said vessel, alive on the morning of August the 6th, 1897, on the left side of the forecastle head of that vessel. It was between nine and ten o'clock of that morning. He was shot at that time and place by John Andersen, the cook of the vessel and the prisoner here. I saw him shoot him. I was at the wheel of the vessel, in the '''wheelhouse''', just aft of the aftercabin.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} The enclosed structure around the [[paddlewheel]] of a [[steamboat]].
##: {{synonyms|en|paddle box}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=P[aul] R[apsey] Hodge|authorlink=Paul Rapsey Hodge|chapter=Part VI. Description of Plates.|title=The Steam Engine, Its Origin and Gradual Improvement, from the Time of Hero to the Present Day; as Adapted to Manufactures, Locomotion and Navigation.{{nb...|[...] With Numerous Explanatory Wood Cuts, and a Volume, Containing Forty-eight Plates.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:D. Appleton & Company|D[aniel] Appleton & Co.]],{{nb...|200 Broadway.}}|year=1840|page=225|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=o6wxAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA225|oclc=726751258|passage=PLATE XXVII.—''Details of the Paddle Wheel of the Steamboat "North America."'' {{smallcaps|Fig. 1}}, shows the outside framing of the paddle-box, or as it is frequently termed, the '''wheel-house'''; also an elevation of the paddle-wheel, shewing the arrangement of the buckets, arms, centre-plate, &c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Gale|editor=E. Meriam|journal=New York Municipal Gazette|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by the Anti-assessment Committee|date=23 April 1845|volume=I|issue=33|page=474|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rtRAAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA4-PA474|column=3|oclc=12891070|passage=The Eureka broke an arm in her larboard '''wheel'''[-]'''house''' with a tremendous crash, tearing the whole structure away. She crawled back to the city to refit.—''Journal Com. April'' 10.}}
# {{lb|en|archaeology}} A [[prehistoric]] structure from the [[Iron Age]] found in [[Scotland]], characteristically including an outer [[wall#Noun|wall]] within which a [[circle#Noun|circle]] of [[stone#Noun|stone]] [[pier]]s ([[resemble|resembling]] the [[spoke#Noun|spokes]] of a wheel) form the basis for [[lintel]] [[arch#Noun|arches]] [[support#Verb|supporting]] [[corbelled#Adjective|corbelled]] [[roofing#Noun|roofing]] with a [[hearth]] at the [[hub]].
# {{lb|en|Canada|US|baseball|by extension from sense 1.2}} A [[pitch#Noun|pitch]] [[location]] which is [[favourable]] to the [[hitter]].
#: {{ux|en|The pitch was right in his '''wheelhouse''', and he hit a grand slam.}}
# {{lb|en|Canada|US|figuratively}} A person's [[area]] of [[authority]] or [[expertise]].
#: {{synonyms|en|domain}}
#: {{ux|en|Horse viruses are in Pat’s '''wheelhouse'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=A. A. Dowd|title={{w|Steven Spielberg}} Finds Fun, and maybe even a Soul, in the Pandering Pastiche of ''[[w:Ready Player One (film)|Ready Player One]]''|url=https://film.avclub.com/steven-spielberg-finds-fun-and-maybe-even-a-soul-in-the-1824094654|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180531213806/https://film.avclub.com/steven-spielberg-finds-fun-and-maybe-even-a-soul-in-the-1824094654|archivedate=31 May 2018|work={{w|The A.V. Club}}|date=26 March 2018|passage=What the film rarely does is challenge or interrogate the fan culture to which it plays uncritical tribute. Wade and his friends, including a trigger-happy cyborgian alpha nerd whose offline identity the film handles more tastefully than the book did, are possessive gatekeepers, viciously protective of their pop-culture '''wheelhouse'''.}}

